hey guys Pete here today will pick up on  the problems in Game of Thrones Season  eight series last time we talked about  the problems with the night king story  and today we'll talk about the Iron  Throne I decided to do this problem  series because the ending is difficult  to figure out for a number of different  reasons focusing on problems as a way to  work through it it's not a series saying  that Game of Thrones Season 8 will be  bad because of this and this and this  actually I'm rooting for the end of the  series to be good and I think it will be  but when trying to sift through all the  textual evidence in the books and what  we've seen in the show so far I find it  hard to see what that good ending  actually looks like a big part of why I  keep running into problems is because we  don't expect the ending to be  predictable the bar has been set pretty  high with strong character development  that has us all plugged in but there's  also quite a lot that fits into common  recurring literary devices for instance  one of the main characters grew up as a  bastard boy who didn't know who his real  parents were he's gone off to live a  life of service protecting mankind  against the threat that they forgot  actually exists turns out said bastard  is actually the heir to the throne with  an impressive bloodline and that threat  that had disappeared for thousands of  years is actually on humanity's doorstep  trying to get in now he's primed for a  hero's journey and that has problems  that we'll discuss in a different video  sometime this week but you get the  picture we have a story that isn't  supposed to be that same fantasy story  but happens to have all the elements of  that fantasy story this presents  problems and let's jump into looking at  the ones that the iron throne itself  presents as we all know the iron throne  is the seat of the king or currently the  queen of the Seven Kingdoms of Westeros  the political intrigue and jockeying for  power around the throne is where we get  the name Game of Thrones for the series  one of the first aha moments you get  reading the books is that wait a minute  everyone is focused on the iron  but the story is really about the threat  of the White Walkers  who is the king isn't really going to  matter when everyone is fighting to  survive this is sort of true but then  you realize that as I said in my last  video the living will triumph over the  dead that means that life has to go on  after the war for the dawn and that  means that the Iron Throne is a factor  in the end of the story as we near the  end of the TV series it's clear that the  throne might not be the most important  thing at the beginning of season 8 but  it is still very important this is the  first problem with the Iron Throne that  it isn't a point of focus for the  characters who are most likely to sit on  it right now the battle what's the night  King and the White Walkers is and should  be the only thing John Daenerys are  thinking about as far as they know  Cersei is on their side in this battle  which means the whole idea of the crown  of the throne is out of the picture when  Jaime arrives in the north they'll find  out about her double-cross but at that  point it's gonna be too late to really  change anything this really hit me as a  problem when I watched the trailer for  the first time the season 8 trailer we  have this existential threat of the  White Walkers and the army of the Dead  and the power of the throne is still so  compelling that Cersei is basically  double-crossing Humanity in order to  hold on to her seat in a way it's hard  to imagine but at the same time you can  kind of see where she's coming from she  really has nothing left if she doesn't  have the Iron Throne  this leads into the second major problem  and that is the power of the throne and  the system itself obviously watching  this series are reading the books or  however you came across the story you  realize that it's not a pro monarchy  kind of piece it isn't saying like look  at this great system of government they  have place in this world you know it's  definitely got problems as long as there  is a throne there will always be people  who are trying to get ahead in that  system and there are always be struggles  for power you could see that in the  history of Westeros and with all the  secession problems that have happened  and all the war and pain and suffering  that's been taking place since this  series started I'm not sure if the show  really points it out as much  it's definitely there it's hinted at but  the small folk have been suffering  tremendously since the war of the five  kings so just to make sure we're on the  same page here this is definitely not a  pro war kind of story  and it's definitely not a pro absolute  monarchy kind of story  so on the one hand we agree that the  throne is important but on the other  hand we can also agree that what it  represents is not a good system of  government when I first really got into  reading theories and you know just sort  of engaging in the fandom around the  Song of Ice and Fire  there was a theory that was popular I  would say it was popular that tyrion  would help to install a democracy and  I'm not sure if it's still popular or if  it you know really is the way people  think things are gonna turn out but it  is a sort of natural conclusion when you  see how the story points out all the  flaws in this system as I mentioned  earlier you know you kind of get that  idea when you're reading that the throne  doesn't matter at all because in the end  it just won't be that important and my  10 things that have to happen in order  to make this series end successfully  video I talked about though that we do  need to understand that there is a  government in place when things are done  and that starts with the Iron Throne but  when there's an iron throne there will  always be some sort of a game of Thrones  that goes along with that and that leads  us into the next problem and that is  that none of the characters who might  end up on the throne necessarily seem  like they would be the right candidate  now I know that george RR martin has  said when talking about the end of the  series and how he knows what he knows  about the end of the series like what he  told the showrunners he said directly i  know who will sit on the Iron Throne I'm  not sure if that is to mislead us as he  used to mislead us but if you take it at  face value he says that someone will  actually be sitting on it when the story  comes to an end  since we're expecting some kind of a  subversion of the whole hero's journey  it makes it really hard to figure out  how that plays out of course we have  Daenerys Targaryen and she has  trajectory that she believes ends with  her sitting on the Iron Throne at the  end taking back what's hers by her  birthright but we actually know that she  is not the legitimate heir now that John  would be neither one of these characters  are necessarily the right fit because  this story is told in a way that really  makes it relatable and great in my  opinion all of these characters have  their problems there's really no one  thing that you could say is like hey  okay well that makes sense if that  person ends up sitting on the throne at  the end of the series I'll feel good  about the way life will carry on in the  Seven Kingdoms  after that all that leads me to believe  that Westeros will be changed I mean  they're in this battle the war for the  dawn it will definitely be changed as a  result of that but how do we get to the  point where we're like okay this makes  sense changing the system of government  it sounds like a good idea at first but  then you know say that out loud you know  that it ends with democracy being put in  place like does that really sound like a  awesome ending not exactly you might  like to believe that the people would be  more involved and that that does sound  more realistic right something that  could work but if you think about it  beyond that you know what has happened  in any of the characters stories that  really puts them into place to get that  going  obviously Daenerys had good intentions  whenever she ruled obviously John has  made some good decisions as far as doing  things for the right reasons  but there's really no situation where  either one of those people rule in a  vacuum and everything that they want to  have happen all their good intentions  play out and I will do problems with  each of those characters individually in  this series later on well I look at that  more directly but it is a big problem  this is the way the show has set up  conflict leading into this hidden battle  with the dead and in order for the story  to come full circle we have to feel good  about what might happen in the future  the last book of the series which will  hopefully get at some point is called a  dream of spring so what is a dream of  spring  actually look like also I'm not  overlooking the fact that we have pretty  good evidence that King's Landing is  going to take a hit if not be destroyed  as a result of what comes after the war  for the dawn of course Daenerys saw the  throne room destroyed in her house of  undying vision and also bran saw that  again later on when he was in the  weirwood net so that probably is going  to happen I guess if we take those  scenes literally though the throne is  actually there whoever's left alive to  sit on the throne could melt it down and  pass on some new form of government but  it's just really kind of difficult to  see where the show is taking us do you  think that any of the characters in the  story now would be a good fit for the  Iron Throne in the system that we have  or do you think that that system has to  be changed in order for us to have a  satisfied ending I do think that things  will be changed I think that part won't  be that difficult obviously don't think  Cersei can remain on the 